<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Balaklava</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cembalo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Balaklava is a former town, now part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sevastopol">Sevastopol</a>, on a narrow sheltered bay in the south-west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>. The Genoese held it as Cembalo from the 14th century until the Ottoman conquest in 1475, and the ruins of their fortress overlook the harbour. In 1854, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a>, it was the British base and gave its name to the battle that included the Charge of the Light <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">Brigade</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/balaklava.jpg" alt="Balaklava" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: en: Denis Kamaev ru: Денис Камаев · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Modern_Balaklava_-_Gulf_of_Balaklava_(16.07.2006_17-10-20).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Balaklava is a settlement on the Crimean Peninsula, now part of the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sevastopol">Sevastopol</a> and the administrative centre of Balaklavsky District. It lies at the entrance to the Balaklava Inlet, above which stand the ruins of a Genoese fortress on a clifftop. It is known in Ukrainian and Russian as Балаклава, in Crimean Tatar as Balıqlava and in Greek as Σύμβολον.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> founded a settlement on the site under the name Symbolon, and it was an important commercial city for them. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was controlled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, who called it Yamboli, and then by the Genoese, who conquered it in 1365 and named it Cembalo. The Genoese built a large trading empire across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, buying slaves in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a> and shipping them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>, a lucrative market contested by the Venetians. The fortress is the subject of Mickiewicz's penultimate poem in his 1826 cycle of Crimean Sonnets.</p><p>In 1475 the Turks conquered Cembalo and renamed it Balyk-Yuva, meaning Fish's Nest, from which the name Balaklava came. Russian troops invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> in 1771, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1768-1774">Russo-Turkish War</a> of 1768 to 1774, and Crimea was annexed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> thirteen years later. Afterwards the Crimean Tatar and Turkish population was forcibly replaced by Greek Orthodox settlers from the Archipelago.</p><p>The town became known for the Battle of Balaclava in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a> and the Charge of the Light Brigade, in which a British cavalry brigade, because of a misunderstanding, was sent up a valley held on three sides by the Russians. About 250 men were killed or wounded and over 400 horses lost, reducing the mounted brigade by two thirds. Alfred, Lord Tennyson commemorated it in verse. The balaclava, a knitted garment covering the head and neck, takes its name from the place, where soldiers first wore it, and several towns in English-speaking countries were named Balaklava later in the 19th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>In 1954 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> was transferred from the Russian Soviet republic to the Ukrainian Soviet republic, and in 1957 Balaklava was incorporated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sevastopol">Sevastopol</a> by the Soviet government and lost its city status. An underground submarine base, built to survive a nuclear strike, was operational until 1993, when it began to be decommissioned; the last Russian submarine left in 1996. Balaklava was one of the most secret residential areas in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, and the base is now a naval museum complex. Monuments in the town commemorate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a> and the Great Patriotic War.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Balaklava” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Over the next century they added Soldaia, now Sudak, in 1365 and <b>Cembalo</b>, now Balaklava.</blockquote><a href="/state/genoese-gazaria">Genoese Gazaria</a></li></ul></section>
